  One clarifying question first, because it changes the output contract more than the word-boundary choice does.

  [1mThe fork:[0m whether [38;5;153m'...'[39m is part of the length budget or added on top of it. With a budget of 10 and input
  [38;5;153m"abcdefghijklmno"[39m, one answer gives [38;5;153m"abcdefg..."[39m (10 chars) and the other gives [38;5;153m"abcdefghij..."[39m (13 chars).

  - [1mEllipsis inside the budget[0m — output is never longer than [38;5;153mmaxLength[39m, so callers can rely on the option as a hard cap
    (table columns, fixed-width logs). Costs 3 characters of real content, and needs a decision for [38;5;153mmaxLength <= 3[39m.
  - [1mEllipsis appended after[0m — keeps [38;5;153mmaxLength[39m characters of actual content, but output can be [38;5;153mmaxLength + 3[39m, so it is
    not a cap. Anything downstream that budgets on width has to know to add 3.

  Once callers depend on either, changing it is a silent behavior break — this is the part that is expensive to revisit,
  unlike the word-boundary question, which is a pure quality-of-output tweak.

  [1mRecommendation:[0m ellipsis inside the budget. "Cut to at most N" is what [38;5;153mmaxLength[39m reads like, and a hard cap is the
  property callers can actually build on.
